            <ArticleTitle>Evaluation of weight loss and metabolic changes in diabetic patients treated with liraglutide, effect of RS 6923761 gene variant of glucagon-like peptide 1 receptor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>595-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jdiacomp.2015.02.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1056-8727(15)00057-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A polymorphism of GLP-1 R (rs6923761) has potential implications in weight loss and metabolic control. We decide to investigate the role of this polymorphism on metabolic changes and weight loss secondary to treatment with liraglutide.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A population of 90 patients with diabetes mellitus type 2 and overweight, unable to achieve glycemic control (HbA1c&gt;7%) with metformin alone that require initiation of liraglutide treatment in progressive dose to 1.8mg/day subcutaneously, was analyzed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Fifty one patients (56.7%) had the genotype GG and 39 (43.3%) patients; GA (30 patients, 33.3%) or AA (9 patients, 10%) (A allele carriers). In patients with both genotypes, body mass index (BMI), weight and fat decreased. The proportion of the mentioned reductions was higher in the variant allele carriers; BMI (-0.59±2.5kg/m(2) vs. -1.69±3.9kg/m(2); P&lt;0.05), weight (-2.78±2.8kg vs. -4.52±4.6kg; P&lt;0.05) and fat mass (-0.59±2.5kg vs. -1.69±3.9kg; P&lt;0.05). Weight reduction after liraglutide treatment was greater in the A-allele carriers by 2.9kg (95% CI: 0.27-5.64). The decrease of basal glucose, HOMA-R and HbA1c was similar in both genotypes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our data showed better anthropometric parameters in overweight diabetic subjects with the variant allele (A) of rs6923761 GLP-1 R polymorphism. A allele carriers had a greater decrease in weight and fat mass after treatment with liraglutide. The present study is a preliminary observation, and its results need to be replicated with a higher number of patients in different populations.</AbstractText>
